course: world
lesson: 113
title: Seasons, terms and holidays
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: The Earth is tilted, like a spinning top leaning to one side. When the northern half leans towards the Sun it gets summer, while the southern half leans away and gets winter. So when Freddie in England has a cold Christmas, his cousin in Australia is at the beach in his togs (swimming costume): December there is summer.
explain: People count seasons two ways. Weather scientists use whole months: in the north spring is March, April and May, summer June to August, autumn September to November, winter December to February. Astronomers (scientists who study the sky) start the seasons at the equinoxes and solstices: spring at about 20 March, summer at the longest day, about 21 June.
explain: Near the equator it is warm all year, so seasons are about rain, not cold: in Kenya there are the "long rains" (about March to May) and the "short rains" (about October to December), and India has the monsoon, a big rainy season from about June to September.
explain: School holidays are a different calendar from the seasons, chosen by each country. English schools have three terms with half-term breaks and about 6 weeks off in summer; many US schools finish in late May; Scottish schools break up in late June; in Ukraine the summer holidays last about 3 months and the school year starts on 1 September, the Day of Knowledge.
explain: Holiday weeks cost more, because lots of families want to go at the same time: a trip that is £800 one week can be £1,200 in half-term, £400 more. Dad calls it "daylight robbery".
fact: The Earth is tilted, so when the northern half leans towards the Sun it has summer and the southern half has winter.
fact: In Australia and New Zealand summer is December, January and February, so Christmas comes in summer.
fact: Weather scientists count spring as March, April and May in the north; astronomers start it at the spring equinox, about 20 March.
fact: At an equinox day and night are about equal; at the summer solstice, about 21 June in the north, we have the longest day.
fact: After the longest day the days slowly get shorter, but the hottest weeks usually come later, because land and sea take time to warm up.
fact: Near the equator the seasons are wet and dry rather than hot and cold; Kenya has long rains from about March to May and short rains from about October to December.
fact: In England the school year has 3 terms, autumn, spring and summer, with a half-term break of about a week in each, and about 6 weeks of summer holiday.
fact: 6 weeks of summer holiday is 6 × 7 = 42 days.
fact: Many US schools finish in late May, Scottish schools break up in late June, and Ukrainian summer holidays last about 3 months.
fact: In the UK the clocks go back one hour on the last Sunday of October, which in 2026 is 25 October; it brings darker afternoons, but winter as weather scientists count it starts on 1 December.
fact: Ukraine's winter holidays run from about late December to mid-January, while English schools usually get about 2 weeks at Christmas.
fact: Holidays cost more in school holiday weeks because many families want them then; £1,200 − £800 = £400 more.
fact: Words people use: "break up" means finish school for the holidays (UK), "togs" means a swimming costume (Australia, Ireland), "jandals" means flip-flops (New Zealand), "toque" means a woolly hat (Canada), "nesh" means feeling the cold easily (north of England) and "daylight robbery" means a price that is far too high.
exercise: Freddie's cousin in Australia says Christmas is hot. Why? | December is summer in the southern half of the Earth | Australia is closer to the Sun than England | Christmas is in July in Australia | Australia has no winter at all
exercise: What causes summer and winter? | The Earth's tilt as it goes round the Sun | The Earth getting closer to and further from the Sun | Clouds blocking the Sun in winter | The Moon pulling warm air away
exercise: Ellie has about 6 weeks of summer holiday. How many days is that? | 42 | 36 | 48 | 60
exercise: How many terms does an English school year have? | 3 | 2 | 4 | 6
exercise: What is half-term in a UK school? | A short break in the middle of a term | The last day of the school year | A test halfway through the year | A half day of lessons every single Friday
exercise: Mason in the USA says, "We get out of school in late May." Isla in Scotland breaks up when? | In late June | In late May too | In mid-August | In early September
exercise: Rosie's mum says spring starts on 1 March and Dad says about 20 March. Who's right? | Both; they use two different ways of counting | Only Mum; spring always starts on 1 March | Only Dad; spring always starts on 20 March | Neither; spring starts in April
exercise: What is the summer solstice in the northern half? | The longest day, about 21 June | The hottest day, about 1 August | The day when day and night are equal | The first day of the school holidays
exercise: In Kenya, near the equator, what are the seasons mainly about? | Rain: long rains and short rains | Snow and ice in the cold winter | Long summer days and short days | Hot summers and freezing cold winters
exercise: When are Kenya's long rains? | About March to May | About June to August | About October to December | All year round, every month
exercise: In 2026, on what date do UK clocks go back? | Sunday 25 October | Saturday 31 October | Sunday 1 November | Sunday 18 October
exercise: When does winter start, as weather scientists count it? | 1 December | The day the clocks go back | 1 November | 1 January
exercise: Lewis's school goes back on 3 September. When does summer end, as weather scientists count it? | 31 August | 3 September | 21 June | 30 September
exercise: The holiday costs £800 one week and £1,200 in half-term. How much more is the half-term week? | £400 | £2,000 | £300 | £1,200
exercise: Why do holidays cost more in school holiday weeks? | Many families want to go at the same time | Hotels pay more tax in those weeks | The weather is always better then | Planes have to fly much further in the holidays
exercise: What is 1 September in Ukraine? | The Day of Knowledge, the first school day | The very first day of the long summer holidays | The middle of the long winter school holidays | The day all the clocks go back for the autumn
exercise: On 21 June Lucy's dad says, "Longest day, it's all downhill from here." Why is the hottest weather still to come? | Land and sea take time to warm up | The Sun gets closer in July | The days get longer after 21 June | The clocks go back in July
test: Freddie: "My cousin says Christmas is on the beach in Oz. He's lying, innit?" What's the truth? | He's telling the truth; December is summer there | He's lying; Christmas is cold everywhere | He's lying; Australians have Christmas in July | He's telling the truth because Australia has no seasons
test: A cartoon says winter comes when the Earth moves far from the Sun. Put it right. | Winter comes from the Earth's tilt, not its distance | Winter comes when the Moon blocks the Sun | Winter comes when the Earth stops spinning | Winter comes when the Sun gets smaller
test: Lewis: "Summer hols are the whole summer." His sister: "We go back on 3 September, summer ends 31 August." What's true? | Holidays and seasons are two different calendars | The holidays always last the whole summer | Summer ends when school starts | Summer ends on 21 June
test: Harriet, a bit nesh: "Clocks go back end of October, so that's winter, then?" What's the best answer? | Not yet; it means darker afternoons, but winter starts 1 December | Yes, winter starts when the clocks go back | No, winter starts in January | Yes, and summer starts when they go forward
test: Mia's dad, tamping (furious): "£1,200 for half-term? It was £800 the week before. Daylight robbery!" How much more is it? | £400 | £800 | £1,200 | £2,000
test: What would happen to Australian seasons if the Earth had no tilt at all? | There would be almost no summer or winter anywhere | Australia would have summer all year | Australia and England would swap seasons | The seasons would get twice as strong
test: Wanjiru's teacher in Kenya talks about the "long rains". What would a visitor be wrong to expect there? | A cold snowy winter like in Canada | Warm weather most of the year | A season with lots of rain | Another rainy season later in the year
test: A Kiwi (New Zealand) cousin texts in December: "Grab your jandals, beach this arvo!" ("arvo" means afternoon.) Why does that make sense? | December is summer in New Zealand | December is spring everywhere on Earth | New Zealand has no winter at all, ever | Jandals are warm winter boots over there
test: A Canadian friend pulls on her toque in January while a friend in Sydney, Australia, wears togs. Why? | It is winter in Canada and summer in Australia | It is summer in both countries | It is winter in both countries | Canada is much closer to the Sun than Australia is
test: Zane in Ukraine says, "Our winter holidays are longer than your two weeks." Is he right? | Yes, about late December to mid-January, longer than 2 weeks | No, Ukraine has no winter holidays | No, both countries have exactly two weeks | Yes, Ukraine has 3 months off in winter
test: Lucy: "Longest day? But summer's only starting!" Her dad says it's all downhill. Who's right about the weather? | Lucy; the hottest weeks usually come after 21 June | Dad; it gets colder from 22 June | Dad; summer ends on 21 June | Neither; 21 June is the hottest day of all
exam: Identify the principal cause of the seasons upon the Earth. | The inclination of the Earth's axis | The variation in distance from the Sun | The obstruction of sunlight by the Moon | The variation in the Sun's size
exam: It is given that a summer vacation endures six weeks. Ascertain the number of days thereby comprised. | Forty-two | Thirty-six | Forty-eight | Sixty
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the season in Australia during December. | It is summer | It is winter | It is autumn | It is the rainy monsoon
exam: Whereas a holiday is priced at £800 in an ordinary week and at £1,200 during half-term, determine the additional cost. | Four hundred pounds | Eight hundred pounds | Three hundred pounds | Two thousand pounds
